-- title:
--   Γ'-invariant chartwise meromorphic pullbacks on a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient
-- statement:
--   The statement is set up over the following data, grouped as follows.
--
--   **Base arithmetic data.** A prime $r$; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal O$ (`hπ`), completeness of $\mathcal O$ for the $\pi$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ of cardinality $r$ (`hres`), and $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is the fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficient ring.** A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, such that $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every $x \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Determinant valuation.** A monoid homomorphism $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u \in \mathcal O^{\times}$.
--
--   **Group data.** A group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, a subgroup $\Gamma \le G$ containing an element $z$ with $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix and $v\!\det(\sigma z) = 2$ (`hcent`) and an element $w$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w) = 1$ (`hodd`); a subgroup $\Gamma'$ consisting exactly of the $x \in \Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ even (`hΓ'`); the homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ obtained from $\sigma$ by passing to the projective group (`hρ`); discreteness, in the form that for every vertex $v$ of the lattice tree of $\mathcal O$ in $K_0$ the stabiliser of $v$ in $\rho(\Gamma')$ is finite (`hdisc`); and cocompactness, in the form that there is a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every $\rho(\Gamma')$-orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   **Integral model and uniformising functorial datum.** A scheme $\mathcal X$ and a proper flat morphism $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$; a family $\Theta$ assigning, to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from pairs $(\psi, d)$ with $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B$ and $d$ a Deligne datum over $B$ (an assignment of a $B$-submodule of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ to every full lattice $M \subseteq K_0^2$, with invertible quotient, monotone, homothety-equivariant and non-degenerate at every prime) to the set of $B$-points of $f$, that is, morphisms $\operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. The hypotheses on $\Theta$ are: naturality in $B$ (`hΘnat`); invariance under the twisted $\Gamma$-action (`hΘinv`), where `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for $g = \sigma\gamma$ relates $(\psi,d)$ and $(\psi',d')$ by $\psi' = \psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(g)}$ and $d' =$ the pullback of $d$ along $g^{-1}$; bijectivity on geometric fibres (`hΘfib`), namely that over an algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-field $k$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and a fixed $\psi$, the maps $\Theta(\psi, \cdot)$ are surjective onto the $k$-points of $f$ and have as fibres exactly the twisted $\Gamma$-orbits; and universality (`hΘuniv`), namely that any other such natural $\Gamma$-invariant family $\rho'$ with values in the nilpotent points of a morphism $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ factors through $\Theta$ by a natural family $u$, uniquely so.
--
--   **Analytic frame.** A field $C$ which is a $K_0$-algebra, complete, algebraically closed and valued in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$; a commutative ring $R$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $C$ an $R$-algebra, compatibly; a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi$, that is an element of $K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$; the frame hypothesis `hF`, stating that $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image the valuation ring $\{v \le 1\}$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R$; rank-one cofinality `hrk` (for $v(x) < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ there is $n$ with $v(x)^n \le v(y)$) and `hval` (every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates $v(y)$ for some $y \ne 0$); exhaustion `hex`, that every point of the Drinfeld upper half-plane $\Omega = C \setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$; and the hypothesis that the ring of functions on $\Omega$ holomorphic on every affinoid is a domain.
--
--   **Uniformisation of adic points.** A map $\Phi$ from adic points (compatible systems of Deligne data over the rings $R/(\pi^{n+1})$) to $R$-points of $f$, subject to the four-part hypothesis `hΦ`: (i) for each $x$ and $n$, the reduction of $\Phi x$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ is $\Theta$ applied to the reduction of $\psi_0$ together with $x.\mathrm{pt}\,n$; (ii) $\Phi$ is surjective; (iii) $\Phi x = \Phi x'$ if and only if $x' = x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; (iv) base change along $R \to C$ is injective on $R$-points of $f$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, and every $C$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ arises by base change from an $R$-point.
--
--   **Generic fibre.** The structure morphism $s_C : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is required (`hsC`) to be the spectrum of $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$, and the pullback $\mathcal X_C = \mathcal X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O} \operatorname{Spec} C$ is assumed integral, so that it has a generic point $\eta$.
--
--   **Clearing, tube and finiteness hypotheses.** `hclear` (pointwise algebraic clearing): for every open $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ containing $\eta$, every section $s \in \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C}(U)$, every adic point $x$ and every $C$-point $q$ of $\mathcal X_C$ whose first projection is the base change of $\Phi x$ and whose second projection is the identity, there are an open $V \subseteq \mathcal X$ through which $\Phi x$ factors, an open $W \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ contained in the preimage of $V$ under the first projection, with $q$ factoring through $W$ and $\eta \in W$, and finite families $\mathrm{num} : \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to \mathcal O_{\mathcal X}(V)$, $\mathrm{cnum} : \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to C$, $\mathrm{den} : \mathrm{Fin}\,m' \to \mathcal O_{\mathcal X}(V)$, $\mathrm{cden} : \mathrm{Fin}\,m' \to C$ such that, writing $D = \sum_j \mathrm{cden}_j \cdot \mathrm{den}_j$ and $N = \sum_i \mathrm{cnum}_i \cdot \mathrm{num}_i$ for the corresponding sections over $W$ (the scalars pulled back along the second projection, the sections along the first), one has $D \ne 0$, the identity $s \cdot D = N$ on $W \sqcap U$, and, in case $q$ factors through $U$, a further open $W' \le W$ through which $q$ factors and on which $D$ is a unit. `hcover` (clearing along a finite family of charts): for every such $U$ and $s$ there are $\ell$ and opens $V_a \subseteq \mathcal X$, $a \in \mathrm{Fin}\,\ell$, such that every adic point $x$ has $\Phi x$ factoring through some $V_a$, and each $V_a$ carries an open $W \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ inside the preimage of $V_a$, containing $\eta$, and families $\mathrm{num}, \mathrm{cnum}, \mathrm{den}, \mathrm{cden}$ over $V_a$ with $D \ne 0$ and $s \cdot D = N$ on $W \sqcap U$. `htube` (tubewise holomorphy of pulled-back sections): for every $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, every open $V \subseteq \mathcal X$ and every $p \in \mathcal O_{\mathcal X}(V)$ there are $m$ and finite sets $L_k, M_k \subseteq C \times C$ with nonzero second coordinates such that, setting $T_g = \mathrm{vertexTube}\,\varpi\,[g] \cup \mathrm{edgeTube}\,\varpi\,[g] \cup \mathrm{vertexTube}\,\varpi\,[g \cdot \mathrm{edgeFlip}]$ and, for each $k$, $S_k = \{z \in T_g : v(\varepsilon) \le v([g]^{-1}z - e)$ for all $(e,\varepsilon) \in L_k$, and $v([g]^{-1}z - e) \le v(\varepsilon)$ for all $(e,\varepsilon) \in M_k\}$ (the action being by the Möbius transformation attached to the class of $g$ in $\mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$): every coordinate $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ of an adic point $x$ lying in $T_g$ with $\Phi x$ factoring through $V$ lies in some $S_k$; conversely, if the coordinate of an adic point $x$ lies in $S_k$ then $\Phi x$ factors through $V$; and for each $k$ there is $F : S_k \to C$, holomorphic on $S_k$ in the sense of `Omega.holOn` (a uniform limit on $S_k$ of uniformly bounded pole-free rational functions), whose value at the coordinate of any adic point $x$ with $\Phi x$ factoring through $V$ is the image in $C$ of the value of $p$ at $\Phi x$. `hfin` (finiteness of exceptional coordinates): for every open $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ containing $\eta$ and every $n$, the set of $z$ in the affinoid $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$ which are coordinates of adic points $x$ admitting a $C$-point $q$ as above (first projection the base change of $\Phi x$, second projection the identity) that does **not** factor through $U$ is finite.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every open $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point $\eta$ and every section $s \in \mathcal O_{\mathcal X_C}(U)$ there exists a function $F : \Omega \to C$ on the Drinfeld upper half-plane $\Omega = C \setminus \mathrm{image}(K_0)$ with the following four properties.
--
--   First, $F$ computes the values of $s$: for every adic point $x$ and every $z \in \Omega$ with $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$, and every $C$-point $q$ of $\mathcal X_C$ with first projection the base change of $\Phi x$ along $R \to C$ and second projection the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, if $q$ factors through $U$ then $F(z)$ is the element of $C$ obtained by pulling $s$ back along $q$ and taking global sections.
--
--   Second, $F$ is $\Gamma'$-invariant: for $\gamma \in \Gamma'$ and $z, w \in \Omega$ with $w$ the image of $z$ under the Möbius transformation of $\rho\gamma$, one has $F(w) = F(z)$.
--
--   Third, $F$ is meromorphic on each affinoid chart: for every $n$ there are $a, b : \mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n \to C$, both holomorphic on that affinoid in the sense of `Omega.holOn`, with $b$ not identically zero, such that $b(z)\,F(z) = a(z)$ for every $z$ in the affinoid at which $b(z) \ne 0$.
--
--   Fourth, $F$ is meromorphic on a small disc about each coordinate of an adic point lying over $U$: for every adic point $x$ and $z \in \Omega$ with $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$, and every $q$ as in the first clause which factors through $U$, there exist $N \in \mathbb N$ such that the closed disc $D = \{w \in C : v(w - z) \le v(\varpi)^{N}\}$ is contained in $\Omega$, and functions $a, b : D \to C$ holomorphic on $D$ with $b$ nonvanishing at the centre $z$ and $b(w)\,F(w) = a(w)$ for all $w \in D$.
--
--   This is the analytic half of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation step: it converts a rational section on the generic fibre $\mathcal X_C$ of the integral model into a single $\Gamma'$-invariant function on the Drinfeld upper half-plane which is meromorphic on every affinoid chart and on small discs around the coordinates of adic points lying over $U$, using the given algebraic clearing data, the tubewise holomorphy of pulled-back sections and the finiteness of exceptional coordinates. It is used by the construction of the evaluation embedding of the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ into the field of $\Gamma'$-invariant chartwise meromorphic functions, and by the corresponding statement under a smoothness assumption.
